00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
it's the hottest day ever recorded in  the uk  and in london alone the fire brigade is  dealing with more than 1 000 incidents  here's how the day unfolded in the worst  hit community in wenington east london  the fire starts at around 1 pm  this video shows resident alfie stock  trying to put out the early stages of  the fire with a garden hose he lives  here at 22 the green he says the fire  started in a compost pile at the house  next door that was empty at the time the  exact cause of the fire is still being  investigated  the london fire brigade received the  first call alerting them to the incident  at 106 pm they arrive seven minutes  later the fire started less than a  hundred meters from wennington fire  station the crews based there were five  miles away in upminster attending  another fire  at 204 pm the sky news helicopter is  heading towards the essex coastline and  spots the fire  alfie stock's house here at number 22  and next door are already gutted and the  fire has spread to number 20.  the wind direction appears to be playing  a big part in how the fire spreads  further it's moving north west pushing  the fire towards more houses  it's been an hour since the fire started  and it's travelled more than 200 metres  west to these gardens ten minutes later  at 2 28 it's reached this house and  completely engulfed the large  outbuilding  in the fields to the south landowners  are trying to stop the spread by  creating a channel in the field using a  tractor and are moving water containers  in to help  but it doesn't work two hours later we  can see the landowners fail to stop the  spread and it's continued to move south  across the fields  and in the village the fire has now  moved 400 meters west five houses on  wellington road in our lights and it's  moving fast nine minutes later a further  two houses have caught fire  in video film the next morning the  grounds of the church are charred but  the building itself remains intact  in total we counted 17 houses severely  damaged plus a number of other buildings  including garages and stables  it took the fire brigade eight hours to  get it under control  the 19th of july 2022 made history for  the wrong reasons the hottest day since  records began the busiest day for the  london fire brigade since world war ii  across the country fire services  declared 14 major incidents and we are  told climate change means this will not  be a one-off  adam parker sky news
 

